      "blocks": {
        "b_balance": {
          "type": "band",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Why balance matters more than cleanliness",
            "body": "<p>Some of those residents earn their keep. Beneficial bacteria such as <em>Staphylococcus epidermidis</em> are credited in the research with helping to hold less welcome microbes in check and with supporting the skin barrier. When the community is thrown out of balance, a state researchers call dysbiosis, studies have associated it with conditions including acne, eczema and rosacea.</p><p>An association in the research is not evidence that a moisturiser treats any of those conditions, and we make no such claim. What it does suggest is that skincare should avoid making things worse.</p>",
            "caveat": "<p>Healthy skin mostly manages its own microbiome. Often the most useful thing a product can do is leave it undisturbed.</p>",
            "variant": "plain",
            "set_piece": "none",
            "ethos_icon": "none"
          }
        },
        "b_support": {
          "type": "band",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Support, not control",
            "body": "<p>Harsh cleansing, high-pH products and heavy fragrance can all disturb the balance, so much of microbiome-friendly formulating is about what you leave out. We make every product slightly acidic (pH 5 to 5.5), because the research indicates the skin's resident microbes prefer it, and we leave out synthetic fragrance to lower the risk of irritation. Of everything we do for the microbiome, this has the strongest evidence behind it.</p><p>Two ingredients play a more active part.</p>",
            "variant": "plain",
            "set_piece": "none",
            "ethos_icon": "none"
          }
        },
        "b_feeding": {
          "type": "band",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Feeding and steering, gently",
            "body": "<p>Prebiotics are food for the microbes already living on your skin. We use <a href=\"/blogs/ingredients/inulin\">inulin</a>, a plant-derived prebiotic, with selective feeding shown in the laboratory but not yet on human skin. We also include a <a href=\"/blogs/ingredients/chlorella-vulgaris-extract\">chlorella extract</a>. Its maker's evidence, from a small study, suggests it holds back the more troublesome species while sparing the helpful ones. That is their data, and we grade it as such.</p><p>It is a young field. A good deal of the microbiome language in skincare runs ahead of the evidence, promising to rebuild or rebalance your skin in ways the studies do not yet support. So we describe these ingredients as support, and make no claim that they rebuild or rebalance anything.</p>",
            "caveat": "<p>Every Biotaderm moisturiser is slightly acidic (pH 5 to 5.5), carries a plant prebiotic, and leaves out synthetic fragrance. The aim is to work with the system your skin already runs, rather than override it.</p>",
            "variant": "plain",
            "set_piece": "none",
            "ethos_icon": "none"
          }
        },
